{
 "id": "common-students",
 "title": "Common Students",
 "pattern": "hashing",
 "level": "easy",
 "statement": "<p>A school admin API stores which students are registered to which teachers. One endpoint answers: \"which students do <b>all</b> of these teachers share?\"</p><p>Write <code>def solution(registrations, teachers)</code>. <code>registrations</code> is a list of <code>[teacher_email, student_email]</code> pairs (a pair may repeat). <code>teachers</code> is a non-empty list of teacher emails.</p><p>Return the sorted list of students who are registered to <b>every</b> teacher in <code>teachers</code>, with no duplicates. Emails are compared exactly, so case matters. With one teacher, that is all of their students. A teacher with no registrations has no students, so the answer is then <code>[]</code>.</p>",
 "examples": [
  {
   "input": "registrations = [[\"teacherken@gmail.com\", \"commonstudent1@gmail.com\"], [\"teacherken@gmail.com\", \"commonstudent2@gmail.com\"], [\"teacherken@gmail.com\", \"student_only_under_teacher_ken@gmail.com\"], [\"teacherjoe@gmail.com\", \"commonstudent1@gmail.com\"], [\"teacherjoe@gmail.com\", \"commonstudent2@gmail.com\"]], teachers = [\"teacherken@gmail.com\", \"teacherjoe@gmail.com\"]",
   "output": "['commonstudent1@gmail.com', 'commonstudent2@gmail.com']",
   "why": "Ken has three students, Joe has two. Only the two they share are returned."
  },
  {
   "input": "registrations = (same as example 1), teachers = [\"teacherken@gmail.com\"]",
   "output": "['commonstudent1@gmail.com', 'commonstudent2@gmail.com', 'student_only_under_teacher_ken@gmail.com']",
   "why": "One teacher: every student registered to Ken."
  },
  {
   "input": "registrations = [[\"teacherken@gmail.com\", \"studentjon@gmail.com\"]], teachers = [\"teacherken@gmail.com\", \"teachernobody@gmail.com\"]",
   "output": "[]",
   "why": "An unknown teacher has no students, so nobody is common to both."
  }
 ],
 "constraints": [
  "0 &le; len(registrations) &le; 50,000",
  "1 &le; len(teachers) &le; 100",
  "emails are compared exactly (case-sensitive); <code>teachers</code> may repeat an email"
 ],
 "starter": "def solution(registrations, teachers):\n    # write your code here\n    pass\n",
 "think": [
  {
   "step": "Understand",
   "q": "What does \"common to all teachers\" mean in set terms?",
   "a": "Each teacher has a set of students. The answer is the <b>intersection</b> of those sets, sorted. Not the union: a student of only one teacher is left out."
  },
  {
   "step": "Small example by hand",
   "q": "Ken: {a, b, c}. Joe: {b, c, d}. Anna: {c, d}. Ask for Ken and Joe, then all three.",
   "a": "Ken &cap; Joe = {b, c}. Ken &cap; Joe &cap; Anna = {c}. Adding teachers can only shrink the answer."
  },
  {
   "step": "Naive idea",
   "q": "What is the obvious loop?",
   "a": "For each student, for each teacher, scan the whole registration list for that pair. That is O(students &times; teachers &times; R). With 50,000 rows it is far too slow, and it re-reads the same data over and over."
  },
  {
   "step": "Which pattern?",
   "q": "Clue words?",
   "a": "\"registered to all\", \"no duplicates\" &rarr; <b>hash map of sets</b>. Group once (teacher &rarr; set of students), then intersect. Python's <code>&amp;</code> on sets does the intersection in time proportional to the smaller set."
  },
  {
   "step": "Plan in plain steps",
   "q": "Write the steps.",
   "a": "<ol><li>Build <code>students_of = defaultdict(set)</code> in one pass.</li><li>Start with a copy of the first teacher's set (or empty if unknown).</li><li>Intersect with each other teacher's set; stop early if it becomes empty.</li><li>Return <code>sorted(common)</code>.</li></ol>"
  },
  {
   "step": "Edge cases to test",
   "q": "What could break?",
   "a": "One teacher. An unknown teacher (must give <code>[]</code>, not crash with a <code>KeyError</code>). The same teacher listed twice. Duplicate registration rows. No registrations at all. Do not mutate the stored set: copy it before <code>&amp;=</code>."
  }
 ],
 "hints": [
  "Group first: a dict from teacher to the <b>set</b> of their students. One pass over <code>registrations</code>.",
  "The answer is the intersection of the sets of the requested teachers. Use <code>.get(teacher, set())</code> so an unknown teacher gives an empty set instead of a <code>KeyError</code>.",
  "Start with <code>set(students_of.get(teachers[0], set()))</code> (a copy), then <code>common &amp;= ...</code> for each remaining teacher, and <code>return sorted(common)</code>."
 ],
 "solution": "from collections import defaultdict\n\ndef solution(registrations, teachers):\n    # one pass: teacher -> set of their students\n    students_of = defaultdict(set)\n    for teacher, student in registrations:\n        students_of[teacher].add(student)\n    # start from the first teacher's set and keep only students every other teacher shares\n    common = set(students_of.get(teachers[0], set()))\n    for teacher in teachers[1:]:\n        common &= students_of.get(teacher, set())\n        if not common:            # nothing left to intersect\n            break\n    return sorted(common)\n",
 "brute": "def solution(registrations, teachers):\n    # For every student, scan the whole list once per teacher: O(R * R * T), slow\n    result = []\n    for _, student in registrations:\n        if student in result:\n            continue\n        if all([t, student] in registrations for t in teachers):\n            result.append(student)\n    return sorted(result)\n",
 "explain": "<p>Example 1, step by step:</p><ul><li>Group: Ken &rarr; {commonstudent1, commonstudent2, student_only_under_teacher_ken}; Joe &rarr; {commonstudent1, commonstudent2}.</li><li>Start with a copy of Ken's set (3 students).</li><li>Intersect with Joe's: 2 left.</li><li>Sort and return.</li></ul><p>Why copy the first set: <code>common &amp;= ...</code> changes the set in place. Without the copy you would silently delete students from Ken's stored data, and the next query would be wrong. This is the <code>GET /api/commonstudents?teacher=...&amp;teacher=...</code> endpoint of the take-home; in SQL it is a <code>GROUP BY student HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT teacher) = n</code>.</p>",
 "complexity": "O(R + sum of the requested sets + K log K) time for R registrations and K results; O(R) extra space",
